<AtTheTable>
  When the question says “hit at least once,” first examine whether “miss every
  time” is easier to count. Also say whether the needed hit is actually
  sufficient to win: having an ace and winning the pot are different events.
  Later drawing-odds lessons will use the same complement mechanism with more
  careful definitions of a useful card.
</AtTheTable>

<QuantCorner>
  The chance that at least one adverse event occurs can be easier to calculate
  through the chance that none occurs. The complement identity itself needs no
  independence assumption. Computing the probability of “none” across several
  events may still require a model of how those events depend on one another.
</QuantCorner>
